---
import LearnArticle from "../../components/LearnArticle.astro";
import DemoFrame from "../../components/DemoFrame.astro";
import { estimateStrength, secondsToCrack, describeDuration } from "../../lib/passwordStrength";

// Worked examples are computed with the exact same estimator the demo uses,
// so the table and the live tool can never disagree.
const FAST = 1e10;
const examples = [
	{ label: "8 random lowercase letters", sample: "kqzmtbwe" },
	{ label: "8 random mixed characters", sample: "Kq7#zM2!" },
	{ label: "4 random words", sample: "orbit tulip gravel sneeze" },
	{ label: "12 random mixed characters", sample: "Kq7#zM2!pX9&" },
	{ label: "6 random words", sample: "orbit tulip gravel sneeze lantern dusk" },
].map((ex) => {
	const est = estimateStrength(ex.sample);
	return { ...ex, bits: Math.round(est.bits), time: describeDuration(secondsToCrack(est.bits, FAST)) };
});
---

<LearnArticle
	href="/learn/password-strength"
	description="What makes a password strong? Type one in to see its entropy and an estimated brute-force crack time, and learn why length beats complexity."
	lead="A strong password isn't about clever symbols — it's about how many guesses it would take to stumble onto it."
>
	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">It's a Guessing Game</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			When someone tries to break into an account, they usually aren't clever — they're fast. A computer
			can try billions of passwords a second, one after another, until one works. This is called a{" "}
			<em>brute-force attack</em>.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			So the real question is: <em>how many possible passwords</em> would an attacker have to try? That
			number is called the <em>guess space</em>.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			You've met this idea already if you've played LogicDash. A 5-bit pattern has 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 =
			<span class="mono-highlight">32</span> possibilities. Each extra bit doubles it. Passwords work the
			same way, just with far more than two choices per position.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block block--example">
		<p class="block-title">Counting the Possibilities</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			For each character in a password, count how many different characters could go there:
		</p>
		<ul class="block-list">
			<li>Only lowercase letters: <span class="mono-highlight">26</span> choices</li>
			<li>Lowercase and capitals: <span class="mono-highlight">52</span></li>
			<li>Add digits: <span class="mono-highlight">62</span></li>
			<li>Add symbols like ! and #: about <span class="mono-highlight">95</span></li>
		</ul>
		<p class="block-text">
			Then multiply once for every character. An 8-letter lowercase password has 26 × 26 × 26 × 26 × 26 ×
			26 × 26 × 26 ≈ <span class="mono-highlight">209 billion</span> possibilities. Sounds like a lot — but
			a fast computer gets through that in seconds.
		</p>
		<p class="block-callout">
			<em>Entropy</em> is the guess space measured in bits: "this password is as hard to guess as a
			random pattern of this many bits." Every extra bit doubles the attacker's work.
		</p>
	</section>

	<DemoFrame label="Password strength demo">
		<div class="pw-demo" data-pw-demo>
			<p class="pw-privacy">
				Everything is worked out on your device — nothing you type is sent anywhere. Even so, don't type
				a password you actually use.
			</p>
			<div class="demo-field">
				<label for="pw-input">Try a password</label>
			</div>
			<div class="pw-input-row">
				<input
					id="pw-input"
					class="demo-input pw-input"
					type="password"
					autocomplete="off"
					autocapitalize="off"
					spellcheck="false"
					maxlength="64"
					data-pw-input
				/>
				<button type="button" class="demo-btn pw-toggle" data-pw-toggle aria-pressed="false">Show</button>
			</div>
			<div class="pw-examples">
				<span class="pw-examples-label">Or try one of these:</span>
				<button type="button" class="pw-example" data-pw-example="password1">password1</button>
				<button type="button" class="pw-example" data-pw-example="P@ssw0rd!">P@ssw0rd!</button>
				<button type="button" class="pw-example" data-pw-example="qwertyuiop">qwertyuiop</button>
				<button type="button" class="pw-example" data-pw-example="Kq7#zM2!">Kq7#zM2!</button>
				<button type="button" class="pw-example" data-pw-example="orbit tulip gravel sneeze lantern">orbit tulip gravel sneeze lantern</button>
			</div>
			<div class="demo-field">
				<label for="pw-attacker">Attacker</label>
				<select id="pw-attacker" class="demo-select" data-pw-attacker>
					<option value="10">Guessing on a website (10 guesses a second)</option>
					<option value="10000">Stolen password file, well protected (10 thousand a second)</option>
					<option value="10000000000" selected>Stolen password file, fast computer (10 billion a second)</option>
				</select>
			</div>

			<div class="pw-meter" aria-hidden="true">
				<div class="pw-meter-fill" data-pw-meter></div>
			</div>
			<p class="pw-verdict" data-pw-verdict>Type a password to begin</p>
			<p class="pw-time-label">Time to crack, on average</p>
			<p class="pw-time" data-pw-time>—</p>

			<div class="demo-stats">
				<div class="demo-stat">
					<span class="demo-stat-value" data-pw-length>0</span>
					<span class="demo-stat-label">Characters</span>
				</div>
				<div class="demo-stat">
					<span class="demo-stat-value" data-pw-pool>0</span>
					<span class="demo-stat-label">Choices each</span>
				</div>
				<div class="demo-stat">
					<span class="demo-stat-value" data-pw-bits>0</span>
					<span class="demo-stat-label">Bits of entropy</span>
				</div>
			</div>

			<ul class="pw-sets" aria-label="Character types used">
				<li data-pw-set="lower">a–z</li>
				<li data-pw-set="upper">A–Z</li>
				<li data-pw-set="digits">0–9</li>
				<li data-pw-set="symbols">!@#</li>
			</ul>

			<ul class="pw-warnings" data-pw-warnings></ul>
			<p class="sr-only" aria-live="polite" data-pw-live></p>
			<p class="demo-note">
				A simple estimate: it counts characters, and spots famous passwords and easy patterns. Real
				attackers have more tricks, so treat this as a best case.
			</p>
		</div>
	</DemoFrame>

	<section class="block block--example">
		<p class="block-title">Length Beats Complexity</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Here's how long a fast computer (10 billion guesses a second) would take, on average:
		</p>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table class="data-table">
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th>Password made of…</th>
						<th>Bits</th>
						<th>Time to crack</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					{
						examples.map((ex) => (
							<tr>
								<td>{ex.label}</td>
								<td class="num">{ex.bits}</td>
								<td>{ex.time}</td>
							</tr>
						))
					}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p class="block-text">
			Adding four more characters did far more than swapping letters for symbols. And six random words —
			easy to picture, easy to type — beat eight characters of keyboard soup by a mile.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">The Shortcuts Attackers Take</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Attackers don't start at "aaaaaaa." They start with lists of passwords that real people use,
			collected from past data leaks — "123456," "password," "qwerty," pets' names, sports teams.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			They also know our habits: a capital letter at the start, a number and "!" at the end, "@" instead of
			"a." Those tricks feel clever, but they add very little, because everyone uses them.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">What Actually Keeps You Safe</p>
		<ul class="block-list">
			<li>
				<em>Go long.</em> A passphrase of four to six random words is both strong and memorable.
			</li>
			<li>
				<em>Never reuse a password.</em> If one website leaks it, attackers will try it everywhere else.
			</li>
			<li>
				<em>Use a password manager.</em> It creates and remembers a different strong password for every
				account, so you only need to remember one.
			</li>
			<li>
				<em>Turn on two-step verification</em> (sometimes called multi-factor authentication). Even if
				someone guesses your password, they'd also need your phone or a code to get in.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>
</LearnArticle>

<script>
	import { estimateStrength, secondsToCrack, describeDuration, verdict } from "../../lib/passwordStrength";

	const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-pw-demo]")!;
	const input = root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("[data-pw-input]")!;
	const toggle = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-pw-toggle]")!;
	const attacker = root.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>("[data-pw-attacker]")!;
	const meter = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-pw-meter]")!;
	const verdictEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-pw-verdict]")!;
	const timeEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-pw-time]")!;
	const lengthEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-pw-length]")!;
	const poolEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-pw-pool]")!;
	const bitsEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-pw-bits]")!;
	const warningsEl = root.querySelector<HTMLUListElement>("[data-pw-warnings]")!;
	const liveEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-pw-live]")!;
	let liveTimer: number | undefined;

	function render() {
		const password = input.value;
		const est = estimateStrength(password);
		const rate = Number(attacker.value);

		lengthEl.textContent = String(est.length);
		poolEl.textContent = String(est.poolSize);
		bitsEl.textContent = String(Math.round(est.bits));
		(["lower", "upper", "digits", "symbols"] as const).forEach((key) => {
			const chip = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-pw-set="${key}"]`)!;
			chip.classList.toggle("is-used", est.sets[key]);
			chip.setAttribute("aria-label", `${chip.textContent}: ${est.sets[key] ? "used" : "not used"}`);
		});

		warningsEl.innerHTML = "";
		est.warnings.forEach((w) => {
			const li = document.createElement("li");
			li.textContent = w;
			warningsEl.append(li);
		});

		if (password.length === 0) {
			meter.style.width = "0%";
			verdictEl.textContent = "Type a password to begin";
			timeEl.textContent = "—";
			return;
		}

		const time = describeDuration(secondsToCrack(est.bits, rate));
		meter.style.width = `${Math.max(4, Math.min(100, (est.bits / 100) * 100))}%`;
		verdictEl.textContent = verdict(est.bits);
		timeEl.textContent = time.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + time.slice(1);

		// Announce after typing pauses, not on every keystroke.
		window.clearTimeout(liveTimer);
		liveTimer = window.setTimeout(() => {
			liveEl.textContent = `${verdict(est.bits)}. About ${Math.round(est.bits)} bits. Time to crack: ${time}.`;
		}, 700);
	}

	input.addEventListener("input", render);
	// Example buttons show the password in plain text — they're for comparing
	// side by side in class, not secrets.
	root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-pw-example]").forEach((btn) => {
		btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
			input.value = btn.dataset.pwExample ?? "";
			input.type = "text";
			toggle.textContent = "Hide";
			toggle.setAttribute("aria-pressed", "true");
			render();
		});
	});
	attacker.addEventListener("change", render);
	toggle.addEventListener("click", () => {
		const show = input.type === "password";
		input.type = show ? "text" : "password";
		toggle.textContent = show ? "Hide" : "Show";
		toggle.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(show));
		input.focus();
	});

	render();
</script>

<style is:global>
	.pw-demo .pw-examples {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 0.4em;
		margin: 0.8em auto 0;
		max-width: 40em;
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-examples-label {
		font-size: 0.88em;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-example {
		min-height: 2.3em;
		padding: 0.25em 0.75em;
		border-radius: 999px;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
		background: var(--color-bg);
		color: var(--color-text);
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.85em;
		cursor: pointer;
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-example:hover,
	.pw-demo .pw-example:focus-visible {
		border-color: var(--color-accent);
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-privacy {
		margin: 0 auto 0.6em;
		max-width: 36em;
		font-size: 0.88em;
		text-align: center;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-input-row {
		display: flex;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 0.5em;
		margin-top: 0.4em;
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-input {
		width: min(100%, 22em);
		font-size: 1.15em;
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-attacker,
	.pw-demo [data-pw-attacker] {
		max-width: 100%;
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-meter {
		margin: 1.1em auto 0;
		max-width: 30em;
		height: 1em;
		border-radius: 999px;
		background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-border) 70%, transparent);
		overflow: hidden;
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-meter-fill {
		width: 0;
		height: 100%;
		border-radius: 999px;
		background: linear-gradient(90deg, #ffae2e, #ffd27a);
		transition: width 0.25s ease;
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-verdict {
		margin: 0.5em 0 0;
		text-align: center;
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 1.15em;
		color: var(--color-text);
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-time-label {
		margin: 0.8em 0 0;
		text-align: center;
		font-size: 0.78em;
		font-weight: 700;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-time {
		margin: 0.1em 0 0;
		text-align: center;
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 1.5em;
		line-height: 1.3;
		color: #fff3d6;
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-sets {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 0.4em;
		margin: 0.9em 0 0;
		padding: 0;
		list-style: none;
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-sets li {
		padding: 0.25em 0.8em;
		border-radius: 999px;
		border: 1px dashed var(--color-border);
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 0.9em;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-sets li.is-used {
		border-style: solid;
		border-color: var(--color-accent);
		color: #fff3d6;
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-sets li.is-used::before {
		content: "✓ ";
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-warnings {
		margin: 0.9em auto 0;
		max-width: 36em;
		padding-left: 1.2em;
		font-size: 0.95em;
		line-height: 1.5;
		color: var(--color-text);
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-warnings:empty {
		display: none;
	}

	.pw-demo .pw-warnings li + li {
		margin-top: 0.3em;
	}

	@media (max-width: 480px) {
		.pw-demo [data-pw-attacker] {
			width: 100%;
			font-size: 0.85em;
		}
	}
</style>
